# day 13 · piece 4 of 6 — ipv4.txt: VALID addresses, octet by octet
#
# An octet 0-255 is an alternation: 25[0-5] | 2[0-4][0-9] | 1[0-9]{2} | [1-9]?[0-9].
# Put it in a shell variable and build the address as $o(\.$o){3}. Guard both ends so
# 1.2.3.4.5 (five parts) and 256.1.1.1 do not leak through — a first grep -o with the
# guards, a second grep -o to strip them. The skeleton takes any four numbers.
#DAY 13
#FILE ipv4.sh
#RUN bash ./ipv4.sh; echo '--- ipv4.txt:'; cat ipv4.txt
#!/usr/bin/env bash
grep -oE '([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}' input.txt       # ← 256.1.1.1 and 1.2.3.4 (of .5) match here; make it valid-only
#> out: 256.
#> out: ipv4.txt: No such file or directory
#> out-not: # exit:    0
#> check: ipv4.txt (256.x, .999 and 1.2.3 rejected
#> check-not: PASS: day 13
